#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054486 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ec5627a-4a37-5a14-9b39-1b43b422171f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vertex and Axis of Symmetry of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "d7d8ce77-98d8-54d6-9684-edde0ba9e1ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-1450" :question/problem "For the parabola shown below, find the vertex and the equation of the axis of symmetry.\n\n![](images/e8/e88db5363a50530228fefa2e69a9a3a0e1a62eb461103441e61af5c1f7eb6c3b.png)" :question/worked-solution "The vertex is the \"turning point\" of the parabola. In a downwards parabola, like the one here, the vertex is the maximum point. So, the vertex of this parabola is $(-2,3)$.\n\nThe axis of symmetry is the vertical line that passes through the vertex. Therefore, the axis of symmetry of this parabola is $x=-2$.\n\n![](images/91/91e9d323945b1520aa36b70f3faf8c801e28b21437cd1008b54e76dae76d5ccb.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054487 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa081d4-f85a-5239-b034-b78894429611" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "7b9c1bbd-6d33-5fb8-a207-02cf71ba8b0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-1451" :question/problem "Find the roots of the parabola shown below.\n\n![](images/87/87ed924d518696b8d9f442df341700aab36b8bedfbdee88269c94e54eb8a4478.png)" :question/worked-solution "The roots of a parabola are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the curve intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-2,0)$ and $(4,0)$.\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=-2$ and $x=4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054488 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054722 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "867af724-0aa0-599a-8a5f-ee753fd19414" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Graph of a Parabola Given Its Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "d43441ff-4ab3-5cfd-ad5d-fbb6f82fa504" :question/math-academy-id "e-1452" :question/problem "Sketch the graph of $y=-x^2+4x-3$." :question/worked-solution "Let's construct a table of values for the function\n\n$$\ny=-x^2+4x-3\n$$\n\nWe fill the first row with the $x$-values $x=0,1,2,3,4$ and then use the formula\n\n$$\ny=-x^2+4x-3\n$$\n\nto compute the corresponding $y$-values in the second row.\n\n| $\\,\\,x\\,\\,$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\,\\,y\\,\\,$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-3$ |\n\nIf we plot those points on a graph, we can start to see the shape of a parabola:\n\n![](images/44/442d3550eb77f837da6875bc3877023db44f9f3ee862b433fa56d2186c352bd2.png)\n\nWe fill out the graph by drawing the parabola through the plotted points:\n\n![](images/e9/e95ebb53e7924dc1e99a92be85e6981def3c1186029c6c620bded4bb797f0c06.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054578 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054685 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae1738d1-3261-5769-ab80-0edcc76ab6f3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Y-Intercept of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "5bb6861a-ea00-5644-bc16-d458cd2bb30f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7375" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9e012ecda75df732e1c96ea4a52762fb085f444f41991ed9a3fe72d7d8e10025.png)\n\nThe equation of the curve above is $y=x^2+2x+k$. Find the value of $k$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we substitute $x=0$ into the given equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x^{2} + 2x + k \\\\\ny &= (0)^{2} + 2(0) + k \\\\\ny &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nmeaning that the curve intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,k)$. Based on the graph, we see that we must have $k=0$ since our parabola intercepts the $y$-axis at\n\n$$\n\\left(0,0\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054685 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae1738d1-3261-5769-ab80-0edcc76ab6f3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Y-Intercept of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4cc27684-d306-5b1a-a65f-84d96866a22e" :question/math-academy-id "q-106046" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd847efd8b91b1c622e93b1ee97035bcee803a79a603aa7b4beee8fae77abf06.png)\n\nThe equation of the curve above is $y=-{x}^{2}+2x+k.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we substitute $x=0$ into the given equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{x}^{2}-x+k \\\\ y & =-(0)^{2}+2(0)+k \\\\ y & =k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nmeaning that the curve intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,k).$ Based on the graph, we see that we must have $k=5$ since our parabola intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ec5627a-4a37-5a14-9b39-1b43b422171f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vertex and Axis of Symmetry of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d78ba40-8f18-5ed2-8881-cee1c3fac15b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "vertex: $(3,-2)$, line of symmetry: $x = 3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052278} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e33f00-363c-5bc8-86e2-fe61adf12a25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "vertex: $(3,-2)$, line of symmetry: $x =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052279} {:answer/id #uuid "2624ad88-1170-5dc1-80fc-42bf11160f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "vertex: $(-2, 3)$, line of symmetry: $y = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052280} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf50adc-dbd8-5437-8181-7aef2cd555ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "vertex: $(2, 3)$, line of symmetry: $x =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052281} {:answer/id #uuid "64b673b9-51b3-53f2-bdda-1a52a9c2b0a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "vertex: $(3, 2)$, line of symmetry: $y = 3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052282}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ae76a4c4-d817-58c1-9753-810921f0228b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052277}] :question/id #uuid "e6ba3114-4b55-54d8-a439-a9e561cd3410" :question/math-academy-id "q-24393" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f57f1223c28fc554909be6bbd2e8eafefa21a886b1a5abb47549f17bfa12e2ab.png)\n\nFor the parabola shown above, find the vertex and the equation of the axis of symmetry."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054685 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae1738d1-3261-5769-ab80-0edcc76ab6f3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Y-Intercept of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fffc2429-ebec-5416-9ec6-594433fbf3de" :question/math-academy-id "q-83561" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca545b153cd04a539f30030c27a90dc4489b3a620e9a0d859bae5b531fcc7144.png)\n\nThe equation of the curve above is $y=\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-3x+k.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we substitute $x=0$ into the given equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-3x+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}(0)^{2}-3(0)+k \\\\ y & =k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nmeaning that the curve intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,k).$ Based on the graph, we see that we must have $k=\\frac{1}{2}$ since our parabola intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$"}]}
